# One simulator, one holder

The iPhone 13 mini is the only agent simulator, and only one agent uses it
at a time. The current holder is in `~/.live-poker-trainer/simulator-lock.json`.
Read it with:

```bash
python3 tools/sim_lock.py status   # exit 0 free, 3 in use
```

## Before touching the mini

Holding the lock is required to boot it, `flutter run` on it, hot-restart it,
install, uninstall, or terminate the app, tap or type with `agent_tap.py`,
or take a screenshot (`xcrun simctl io …`).

```bash
SIM_LOCK_RUN_ID="$(python3 tools/sim_lock.py claim --owner <agent-name> --purpose "<what for>")" \
  || { echo "iPhone 13 mini busy"; exit 0; }
export SIM_LOCK_RUN_ID
```

- **Busy means stop.** If the claim exits 3, do not wait, poll, retry, or use
  another simulator. Tell the user who holds it (the claim prints that), skip
  the simulator part of the task, and finish the rest. `/ui-design-agent`
  ends its run.
- Keep `SIM_LOCK_RUN_ID` exported for every later shell call. If the shell
  lost it, re-export the id you were given. Never read another run's id out
  of the lock file.
- Before each direct `xcrun simctl` call, run `python3 tools/sim_lock.py guard`.
  `agent_tap.py` and `refresh-simulator.sh` check the lock themselves.
- During long work that does not touch the mini (implement, tests, merge),
  run `python3 tools/sim_lock.py heartbeat --purpose "<step>"` at least
  every 30 minutes. A lock with no heartbeat for two hours, or whose
  `--pid` exited, is stale and the next agent may claim it.

## When done

```bash
python3 tools/sim_lock.py release   # uses $SIM_LOCK_RUN_ID
```

Release on every exit path, including failures. Do not release another
holder's lock. `release --force` is for the user only, when they ask.

`make-change` step 9 needs no claim of its own: `refresh-simulator.sh`
uses your lock when you hold it, holds it briefly when it is free, and
skips the refresh when someone else holds it.
